#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:1
msgid "Device Operations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:3
msgid ""
"`DeviceOp` describes GPU work on the host. Tensor constructors, kernel "
"launchers, and memory copies return operations that you can compose with "
"`.then()`, `.shared()`, `zip!`, and `unzip`. Run the resulting graph with "
"`.sync()` or `.await`, or capture it for replay with `.graph()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:10
msgid "The DeviceOp Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:12
msgid ""
"Your Rust code runs on the CPU (the *host*) and schedules work on the GPU "
"(the *device*). A `DeviceOp` is how you express that scheduling: the host "
"constructs and composes operations, the device executes them in parallel "
"when the runtime asks it to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:14
msgid ""
"![Host-device execution: how kernel calls flow from the host to the GPU](../"
"_static/images/async-host-device.svg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:14
msgid "Host-device execution: how kernel calls flow from the host to the GPU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:16
msgid ""
"A `DeviceOp` is a lazy description of GPU work — nothing runs until you say "
"so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:18
msgid ""
"let z = api::zeros(&[64, 64]);  // DeviceOp<Output=Tensor<f32>>. No GPU work "
"yet.\n"
"let result = z.await?;          // NOW it executes.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:23
msgid ""
"Every `DeviceOp` implements `IntoFuture`, so every operation is awaitable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:25
msgid ""
"pub trait DeviceOp: Send + Sized + IntoFuture\n"
"where Self::Output: Send { /* ... */ }\n"
msgstr ""

