#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:94
msgid ""
"GPU work starts at `execute()`, which fires during the *first poll* — not at "
"`.await` itself. `.await` is cheap; the DeviceFuture is built immediately at "
"`into_future()`, and actual submission to the GPU happens when the runtime "
"polls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:96
msgid ""
"Calling `.sync()` after every kernel leaves gaps while the CPU waits for "
"completion and submits the next launch:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:99
msgid ""
"CPU:  [launch] [wait......] [launch] [wait......] [launch] [wait......]\n"
"GPU:           [kernel████]          [kernel████]          [kernel████]\n"
"                          ↑                      ↑\n"
"                     idle gap                idle gap\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:106
msgid ""
"These gaps can dominate inference when kernels take only microseconds. A 22-"
"layer transformer with 6 kernels per layer has 132 sync gaps per token. "
"Compose the operations and synchronize once to eliminate those gaps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:110
msgid ""
"let result = rms_norm(out1, hidden.clone(), weight.clone(), eps)\n"
"    .first()\n"
"    .unpartition()\n"
"    .shared();\n"
"\n"
"let q = matvec(out2, result.clone(), wq.clone())\n"
"    .first()\n"
"    .unpartition()\n"
"    .shared();\n"
"\n"
"let output = q.sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:124
msgid ""
"CPU:  [build graph...] [launch all]  [wait]\n"
"GPU:                    [norm████][mv████][add████]\n"
"                         no gaps — work is pipelined\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:130
msgid ""
"cuTile offers three execution modes for running a constructed `DeviceOp`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:132
msgid ""
"**Synchronous** — `.sync()` and `.sync_on(&stream)` block the thread until "
"the result is ready. Best for scripts, debugging, and learning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:134
msgid ""
"kernel(args...).sync()?;            // default device, default stream "
"policy\n"
"kernel(args...).sync_on(&stream)?;  // explicit stream\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:139
msgid ""
"**Asynchronous** — `.await` is non-blocking in an async context. Composes "
"lazily for overlap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:141
msgid ""
"let result = kernel(args...).await?;\n"
"\n"
"let result = step1(args)\n"
"    .then(|out| step2(out))\n"
"    .then(|out| step3(out))\n"
"    .await?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:150
msgid ""
"**CUDA graph** — `.graph()` / `.graph_on(stream.clone())` captures a "
"composed `DeviceOp` into a reusable `CudaGraph<T>`. Best for hot paths that "
"run the same pipeline many times. See [CUDA Graphs](#cuda-graphs) below for "
"the capture/replay pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:154
msgid "Composing DeviceOps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:156
msgid ""
"`DeviceOp`s compose into computation graphs using four combinators: "
"`.then()`, `zip!`, `unzip`, and `.shared()`. Graphs are constructed lazily "
"and evaluated as a unit, which preserves ordering, avoids duplicate work "
"through sharing, and lets independent work overlap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:158
msgid ""
"![Lazy computation graph showing how DeviceOps compose](../_static/images/"
"computation-graph.svg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:158
msgid "Lazy computation graph showing how DeviceOps compose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:160
msgid ""
"**`.then()`** chains operations sequentially — the output of one feeds the "
"next. Chained ops share a stream, so ordering is strict."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:162
msgid ""
"let result = matmul(z, x, y)\n"
"    .then(|(z, _x, _y)| activation(z))\n"
"    .then(|(z,)| normalize(z));\n"
"\n"
"let output = result.await?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:170
msgid ""
"**`zip!`** combines multiple independent `DeviceOp`s into a single tuple-"
"valued one (fan-in). **`unzip`** is the inverse: splits a tuple-producing op "
"into independent branches (fan-out)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:172
msgid ""
"  zip! (fan-in)                     unzip (fan-out)\n"
"\n"
"    op_a ─┐                           ┌── branch_a\n"
"           ├─ zip! ─── (a, b)    (a, b) ── unzip ──┤\n"
"    op_b ─┘                           └── branch_b\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:180
msgid ""
"When you `unzip`, the upstream operation runs **at most once** regardless of "
"how many branches consume it. An internal shared gate executes it on the "
"first branch to poll and caches the results for the rest:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:182
msgid ""
"let (z, x, y) = zip!(z_op, x_op, y_op)\n"
"    .then(my_kernel)\n"
"    .unzip();\n"
"\n"
"let output = z.unpartition().to_host_vec().sync()?;  // kernel runs once\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:190
msgid ""
"**`.shared()`** converts any `DeviceOp` into a `Clone`-able "
"`SharedDeviceOp<T>`. All clones share one `Arc` of the result; the "
"underlying op executes once. Use it when many downstream kernels need the "
"same input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:192
msgid ""
"let x = api::ones(&[32, 32]).shared();\n"
"\n"
"let a = kernel_a(x.clone()).sync()?;  // x executes here (once)\n"
"let b = kernel_b(x.clone()).sync()?;  // Uses the cached Arc\n"
"let c = kernel_c(x).sync()?;          // Also uses the cached result\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:200
msgid ""
"Diamond (fan-out then fan-in) and broadcast are two common composition "
"patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:202
msgid ""
"Diamond (fan-out then fan-in):\n"
"\n"
"  op_a ─┐              ┌─ transform_a ─┐\n"
"         ├── zip! ── unzip              ├── zip! ── result\n"
"  op_b ─┘              └─ transform_b ─┘\n"
"\n"
"Broadcast (.shared() into parallel kernels):\n"
"\n"
"                     ┌── kernel_a ── result_a\n"
"  x.shared() ──────┤\n"
"                     ├── kernel_b ── result_b\n"
"                     └── kernel_c ── result_c\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:217
msgid ""
"The execute-once mechanism assumes sequential polling from a single thread, "
"which is the normal mode for `cuda-async`. Polling both sides of an `unzip` "
"from different OS threads is unsafe (the gate uses a non-atomic check-then-"
"act). Device contexts are thread-local, so the common triggering patterns "
"fail earlier; still, avoid designs that fan out across threads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:221
msgid "Streams and Scheduling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:223
msgid ""
"A CUDA stream is an ordered queue of GPU work. Operations on the same stream "
"execute in submission order; operations on different streams may overlap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:226
msgid ""
"By default, cuTile distributes operations across a pool of 4 streams using a "
"round-robin policy, so independent operations land on different streams and "
"can overlap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:228
msgid ""
"                         ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐\n"
"  Your Code              │          GPU (4-stream pool)            │\n"
" ─────────────           │                                         │\n"
"                         │  Stream 0: ████████                     │\n"
"  op_a.await  ──────────►│  Stream 1:    ████████                  │\n"
"  op_b.await  ──────────►│  Stream 2:       ████████               │\n"
"  op_c.await  ──────────►│  Stream 3:          ████████            │\n"
"  op_d.await  ──────────►│  Stream 0:             ████████         │\n"
"  op_e.await  ──────────►│                                         │\n"
"                         └─────────────────────────────────────────┘\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:241
msgid ""
"Operations serialize in four cases: wrap-around onto the same stream (every "
"4th op in the default pool); chained with `.then()` (same stream); pinned to "
"a single stream via `.sync_on(&stream)`; or awaited sequentially (the host "
"blocks between awaits, so the next op is submitted only after the previous "
"completes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:243
msgid ""
"Operations overlap when they land on different streams **and** are submitted "
"before the host waits for either — typically via `zip!`, `tokio::join!`, or "
"direct lazy composition that the async runtime polls concurrently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:245
msgid ""
"Data dependencies are your responsibility. The round-robin policy does not "
"track them. If operation B reads A's output, you must force ordering:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:247
msgid ""
"// Chain with .then() — same stream, automatic ordering\n"
"let result = create_tensor().then(|t| process(t)).await?;\n"
"\n"
"// Await sequentially — host ensures ordering\n"
"let tensor = create_tensor().await?;\n"
"let result = process(tensor).await?;\n"
"\n"
"// Pin to the same stream — CUDA guarantees ordering\n"
"let stream = device.new_stream()?;\n"
"let tensor = create_tensor().sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
"let result = process(tensor).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:261
msgid ""
"The unsafe pattern — feeding A's output to B but submitting them as "
"independent futures that land on different streams — can produce stale or "
"partial reads:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/device-operations.md:263
msgid ""
"// ⚠️ DANGER: op_b may start before op_a finishes if they land on different "
"streams!\n"
"let future_a = op_a.into_future();                    // Submitted to Stream "
"0\n"
"let future_b = op_b_reads_a_output.into_future();     // Submitted to Stream "
"1\n"
"let (a, b) = tokio::join!(future_a, future_b);\n"
msgstr ""

